WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:03.700
Production site elements. Know what you are standing next to.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:47.820
Every production pad you pull onto is built out of the same handful of pieces. Different operator, different pad, different year, different shade of green paint. Same fifteen things doing the same fifteen jobs. If you have ever pulled onto a strange pad, looked out the window, and thought what in the world is that thing, this is for that. Six of these you never touch. Those belong to the operator, and you keep your hands off them. The other nine you work with. You load through them, you ground to them, you read them, and in an emergency you use them. One more thing before we start. Most of this equipment goes by three or four different names depending on who you are hauling for, so I will give you the ones you are actually likely to hear. The name on the SOP is not always the name on the pad.

3
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:48.960
Well heads.

4
00:00:48.960 --> 00:01:49.580
The wellhead is where all of it starts. You will hear it called a Christmas tree, a well tree, or just the tree, and on paperwork a surface wellhead or a master valve assembly. Whatever it is called, it is the component at the surface of the well that provides the structural and pressure containing interface for the production equipment. On a multi well pad you will see a row of them, sometimes ten or more, each one a stack of flanged valves and fittings that somebody a long time ago decided to call a Christmas tree. The oil field has never been known for subtlety. What is behind that steel is stored pressure, thousands of pounds of it, holding back gas and liquids. Your hazards are leaks and washouts, and the release of flammable gas, vapors, or liquids. This is the first of the six you never touch. Nobody operates a wellhead but the operator's people. You do not touch valves, you do not lean on piping, and you do not park where you would block access to that row.

5
00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:50.580
Line heater.

6
00:01:50.520 --> 00:02:44.160
A line heater does what the name says. Depending on the pad you will hear it called an indirect fired heater, a water bath heater, a glycol bath heater, or just a production heater. They all do the same job. It warms the gas stream before it goes through separation and pressure reduction. Cold gas dropping pressure forms hydrates, which are ice plugs, and an ice plug will choke a line right off. Yes, ice. In a gas line. In August. The heater keeps the stream warm enough to prevent that and to hold separation temperatures where they run efficiently. Two things to know. One, it is a pressure vessel, so stored pressure, leaks, and washouts are all in play. Two, and this is the big one, it has a flame burning inside it. That is a live ignition source sitting out in the open on your pad. Any vapor release drifting toward a line heater is a fire looking for a place to happen. Another one you never touch.

7
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.160
G P U.

8
00:02:45.060 --> 00:03:39.860
GPU stands for gas production unit. The same box gets called a separator skid, a three phase separator, or a heater separator, and which name you hear mostly depends on who trained the person you are talking to. It regulates well stream flow, pressure, and temperature, and pulls liquids out of the gas before it goes to the pipeline or on for further processing. Think of it as the heating and the separation packaged together, usually skidded up in a housing with piping running out both ends. Same hazard set as the line heater. Stored pressure, leaks and washouts, release of flammable gas, vapors, or liquids, and an internal flame burner acting as an ignition source. Not yours to operate. But on most pads the GPU area also has an ESD station, so learn where that is the first time you pull in, not the first time you need it.

9
00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:40.860
Combustor.

10
00:03:40.840 --> 00:04:33.040
A combustor is a gas combustion device that burns off gas the site cannot otherwise capture. You will hear it called an incinerator, an enclosed flare, a thermal oxidizer, a V C U for vapor combustion unit, or just the flare stack. Some run with a visible flame on top. Some are enclosed, and all you see is a big quiet can with a little heat shimmer coming off it. The enclosed ones are the ones to respect, because your brain files them as scenery. Assume it is lit. It is the single largest deliberate ignition source on the pad. Your hazards are that internal flame burner, plus leaks and the release of flammable gas, vapors, or liquids around the unit itself. You do not operate it, but you absolutely work around it. Know where your combustor sits relative to where you are loading, and know which way the wind is carrying your vapors. You never want them headed for that stack.

11
00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:34.160
V R U.

12
00:04:34.160 --> 00:05:25.800
A vapor recovery unit captures vapors that would otherwise be released to the atmosphere or flared off. You might also hear flash gas compressor or flare gas recovery compressor. It is built from a scrubber, a reciprocating compressor, a motor, and switchgear. A pressure switch reads the pressure inside the tanks and turns the compressor on and off, which means a VRU sitting there dead quiet can fire up on its own while you are standing next to it, and it will not ask first. Hazards. Stored pressure, leaks and washouts, release of flammable gas, vapors, or liquids, and the compressor, motor, and switchgear are all potential ignition sources. That is electrical and mechanical ignition right in the middle of your tank area. Not yours to touch. Give it room, and do not use it as a shelf.

13
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:26.820
V R T.

14
00:05:26.820 --> 00:06:05.200
A vapor recovery tower is the tall narrow pressure vessel standing between the production separator and the liquid storage tanks. It also goes by flash separator, degassing tower, or low pressure stripper. Its job is to collect the remaining vapors off produced liquids so they can be recovered and discharged into a sales line by the vapor recovery unit instead of going to the atmosphere. It is the last designated pressure vessel in line. Here is the trap. A VRT looks like a piece of structural steel somebody forgot to finish. It is not. It is under pressure. That makes it the last of the six you never touch, and it does not get climbed, tied off to, or bumped.

15
00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:06.260
Load out pumpkin.

16
00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:48.840
Now we get to the nine you actually work with, and this is the one your hands are on most. The load out valve sits inside that container at the production tank battery or the lay down tank load out area. Everybody calls it the pumpkin. You will also hear containment bucket, load out catch basin, drip pot, or spill pot. Nobody remembers who named it a pumpkin. Look at one and you will not need it explained. The container is there to catch the small amount of fluid that gets lost during loading or transferring. Hazards. Leaks and faulty valves, environmental spills, release of flammable vapors or liquids, and trip hazards created by the hose and piping running out of it. This is where your habits show. Check the valve before you trust it, and close the lid when you are done.

17
00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.540
Production tank battery.

18
00:06:50.540 --> 00:07:44.560
A production tank battery, or just a battery, is the group of tanks connected together to receive crude oil and production fluid from a well or a producing lease. You will hear tank farm, storage battery, stock tanks, gun barrel, or produced water and crude tanks, depending on the customer and what is in them. It is where most of your working time on the pad gets spent. Hazards. Leaks of stored fluids, over pressurization of tanks, release of flammable gas, vapors, or liquids, and trip hazards created by the hoses and pipes crossing that containment. Two things here are not negotiable. First, you never climb over a containment wall. You use the stairs provided, every time, both directions. Second, over pressurization is the one people underestimate. A tank is a bucket with a lid on it. It is built to hold volume, not pressure. Anything that blocks a hatch or a vent line turns that bucket into a pressure vessel, and it was never designed to be one.

19
00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.540
Truck vent line hookup.

20
00:07:46.540 --> 00:08:49.640
The truck vent line hookup is the connection point designed so trucks can properly vent during the loading or unloading process. You will also see it called a vapor balance line, an equalization line, a vent header connection, or a return vapor line. You will find it at the production tank battery and the lay down tank load out areas, and on some sites the vent tie in sits inside the load out pod, the pumpkin itself. Here is the part that matters. How you vent depends on the energy customer's SOP for that site. Nearly all of our customers vent atmospherically, directed away from the truck. Some require the designated vent line. Same trailer, same product, different rule on the other side of the fence line. Welcome to the oil field. You follow the SOP for the pad you are standing on, not the one you ran yesterday. Hazards. Leaks, vapor release, and trip hazards created by hose and pipe. Whichever method the site uses, the point does not change. Vapor goes away from your truck, away from your ignition sources, and away from you.

21
00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:51.040
Truck grounding station.

22
00:08:51.040 --> 00:09:39.000
The truck grounding station is the post and horizontal bar at the load out area, marked attach ground wire here. It also goes by static ground point, earth grounding post, or bonding clamp station. You ground to it before you load, every time, because moving fluid builds static, and static is an ignition source you never see coming. The hazards here are a little different from the rest of the list. Damaged equipment, trip hazards from excessive or loose wire, and struck by or against that horizontal grounding bar, which sits at exactly the height somebody apparently set aside for shins. Inspect the clamp and the wire. Metal to metal contact, or it is not a ground. A connection that looks good but is not making metal to metal is worse than no ground at all, because you will trust it.

23
00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.160
R T U panel.

24
00:09:40.160 --> 00:10:43.520
An RTU, or remote terminal unit, is the panel that ties the equipment on the pad into the operator's SCADA system. Officially you will see it called the SCADA panel, the wellhead control panel, or a PLC enclosure. To every driver who has ever pulled onto a pad, it is the tank screen. That is the name that will actually get you an answer if you ask somebody where it is. It transmits telemetry data to a master system and takes messages back from it, which is how somebody sitting in an office an hour away knows your tank level and can shut equipment in without ever putting a boot on the gravel. You will use it too. Reading your tank levels off that screen before you load and after you load is part of the job, and that number is what protects you when there is ever a question about what you pulled. Reading it is yours. Changing it is not. Nothing on that panel gets opened, reset, or reconfigured by a hauler. It is also electrical equipment, which makes it a potential ignition source, and the conduit and piping running to it creates trip hazards.

25
00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:45.280
Flowback lay down tanks.

26
00:10:45.280 --> 00:11:34.400
Flowback lay down tanks are the trailer style tanks placed on a pad during flowback operations, connected together to receive crude oil and production fluid from a flowing well. You will hear frac tanks, five hundred barrel mobile tanks, flat tops, or axle tanks. You will be pulling from them. They are temporary, and that is exactly why they deserve more attention than the permanent equipment, not less. The layout changes, the hoses run across the ground instead of in racks, and the footing is whatever the pad gives you that week. The route you walked last week is not the route this week. Hazards. Environmental spills, over filling of tanks, leaks of stored fluids, trip hazards created by hose and piping, and release of flammable gas, vapors, or liquids. Walk it before you are carrying anything.

27
00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:35.820
Lay down tank load out.

28
00:11:35.820 --> 00:12:22.020
The lay down tank load out is the area set up to load produced fluids during the well turn in process. It also goes by temporary load out header, temporary suction manifold, or frac tank pump station. It consists of containment, load out pumpkins, load out lines, and vent lines. Venting here follows the same principle as the battery. Whatever the customer's SOP calls for on that site is what you do, and on some sites that tie in is inside the pumpkin itself. Hazards. Leaks and faulty valves, environmental spills, release of flammable vapors or liquids, and trip hazards created by hose and containment. Containment is built to catch a spill. It is equally good at catching a boot, especially at four in the morning.

29
00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:23.020
E S D.

30
00:12:22.900 --> 00:13:06.680
ESD stands for emergency shut down. You will hear emergency shutdown system, shutdown station, or just the panic button. It is a button, usually behind a red and white sign, and it shuts down the wellheads to minimize the consequences of an emergency. An escape of natural gas, an outbreak of fire, an explosion. You will find ESD stations at the muster area, at the GPU area, at the production tank battery, and other areas on the pad. This one is yours to use. Any time a gas leak, fire, or explosion could possibly occur, that button gets used immediately. Not after you check. Not after you call somebody. Immediately. Then you go. Find the nearest ESD every single time you pull onto a pad.

31
00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:07.920
Muster area.

32
00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:47.100
The muster area is the location where everyone assembles after evacuating due to an emergency. It also goes by muster point, assembly point, rally point, or safe refuge zone. It is located at the mouth or beginning of the pad entrance. Two things to lock in. First, in the event of an LEL alarm, shut down your truck and your equipment, then proceed to the muster area on foot. Second, and this is the reason why. Do not drive or operate vehicles and equipment in a potentially flammable environment. A starter, an alternator, hot exhaust. Your truck is an ignition source with a seat in it. Leave it shut down and walk out.

33
00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:48.100
Last thing.

34
00:13:48.000 --> 00:14:34.920
Fifteen pieces of equipment, and nearly all of them come back to the same four things. Stored pressure, flammable product, ignition sources, and the hoses and pipes under your feet. Six of them you never touch. The wellhead, the line heater, the GPU, the combustor, the VRU, and the VRT. Those are the operator's. The other nine you work with. You load through them, you ground to them, you read them, you pull from them, and in an emergency you use them. All fifteen you have to recognize, whatever name that pad calls them by, because you have to know what each one is holding, and where your ESD and your muster area are, before you ever step out of the truck. Different operator, different paint, different names, same fifteen things. Pull on, look around, and know what you are standing next to.

